# Grade 10: teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'10-1-1': dict(
  saint="Andrew: the first to be called, who kept bringing people to Jesus.",
  tryit=["Build the case that Jesus founded a community.", "Cite one text for each claim.", "Rate which is strongest."],
  saintStory="Andrew was a fisherman from Bethsaida, on the Sea of Galilee, who had been a disciple of John the Baptist. When John pointed to Jesus and said, “Behold, the Lamb of God,” Andrew followed, spent the day with Jesus, and then went looking for his brother Simon. “We have found the Messiah.” He brought Simon to Jesus. That is why the Eastern Churches call Andrew the “First-called.”\nHe appears at three other moments, and each time he brings someone. Before the feeding of the five thousand, it was Andrew who pointed to a boy with five barley loaves and two fish: “What are they among so many?” When some Greeks wanted to see Jesus, they went to Philip, who went to Andrew, and the two of them brought them to Jesus.\nTradition says he preached in Greece and Asia Minor and was crucified at Patras on an X-shaped cross. He is patron of Scotland, Russia and Greece. He was a man who was never the leader, but whose work was to introduce others to Christ.",
  script=dict(
    open="Put the question on the board: “Did Jesus found a church, or did the Church just happen?” Ask students to take a side, then say, “We will look at the evidence like historians.”",
    teach=["Say: “Some historians and many people today say that Jesus never meant to start an institution, only to preach the Kingdom, and that the Church was an invention after his death. The Catholic Church says Jesus intended a community with structure, authority and continuity. The evidence from the Gospels: Jesus gathered disciples and chose the Twelve, ‘to be with him and to be sent out.’ Twelve, as in the twelve tribes: he was constituting a renewed Israel (Mark 3:13-19). He gave them a mission, a share in his authority, and a promise.”", "Say: “Key texts. Matthew 16:18-19: ‘You are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church’; the word ‘ekklesia’ (assembly, church) is used by Jesus himself. The keys, binding and loosing. Matthew 18:17-18: the community has authority to settle disputes. John 21:15-17: ‘Feed my lambs… tend my sheep.’ Luke 22:19: ‘Do this in remembrance of me,’ the institution of a continuing ritual. John 20:21-23: ‘As the Father has sent me, even so I send you,’ with power to forgive sins. Matthew 28:19-20: the commission to baptize and teach all nations ‘to the close of the age,’ which implies a community that will last long after the apostles die.”", "Say: “The Church emerges at Pentecost, but is prepared throughout Jesus’ life. Vatican II says: ‘The Lord Jesus inaugurated his Church by preaching the Good News.’ (LG 5) The Catechism describes stages: prepared in the Old Testament, founded by Jesus, revealed by the Holy Spirit at Pentecost, and to be completed in glory. The Church is not a human invention but a gift. Some critics point out that Jesus expected the Kingdom soon. The Church responds that the Kingdom is both present and future, and that the Church is its seed. Peter’s role is also shown by the early record: he is first among the Twelve in every list, speaks first at Pentecost, and presides at the Council of Jerusalem.”"],
    ask=["What did Jesus do to found the Church? (Chose the Twelve, gave them authority and a mission, and promised to be with them.)", "What word does Jesus himself use in Matthew 16:18? (Church, ekklesia.)", "How does the Church describe her origin? (Prepared in the Old Testament, founded by Christ, revealed at Pentecost.)"],
    practice=["Build a case file with six claims: Jesus chose the Twelve, gave Peter a special role, gave authority to bind and loose, instituted the Eucharist, commissioned the disciples, promised to remain.", "For each, write the Bible verse and how you would argue it.", "Rate which is strongest and which weakest.", "Write a short paragraph answering a skeptic."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, you built your Church on the rock. Keep us faithful.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Whom could you bring to Jesus, as Andrew did?”")),

'10-1-2': dict(
  saint="Francis de Sales: a bishop who held that holiness belongs in every walk of life.",
  tryit=["Define “sacrament” and “mystery.”", "Give one example of each in the Church.", "Explain how the Church is both visible and spiritual."],
  saintStory="Francis de Sales was born in 1567 in Savoy, in the Alps. He studied law in Padua and was expected to have a brilliant career, but he chose to be a priest. In 1594 he volunteered for a dangerous mission to the Chablais, a region around Lake Geneva that had become Calvinist. People shut their doors on him. He trudged through snow, once sleeping in a tree to escape wolves. When nobody would listen, he wrote short pamphlets explaining the Catholic faith and slipped them under doors. Slowly, thousands returned to the Catholic Church.\nHe was made bishop of Geneva in 1602 and lived in Annecy. He was gentle, patient and cheerful: “A spoonful of honey attracts more flies than a barrel of vinegar.” In his book Introduction to the Devout Life, he taught that holiness is not just for monks, but for soldiers, workers and mothers. With Jane de Chantal, he started the Visitation sisters.\nHe died in 1622 and is the patron of writers. He is a Doctor of the Church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say, “Think of a wedding ring. It’s a piece of metal. What else is it? How can a physical object be more than it appears?” Take answers. “That is the idea of a sacrament.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church is a mystery. In Catholic usage, mystery doesn’t mean a puzzle to solve but a reality with a divine depth that we can enter but never exhaust. Vatican II’s Lumen Gentium begins: ‘The Church, in Christ, is in the nature of a sacrament, a sign and instrument of communion with God and of the unity of the entire human race.’ A sacrament is a visible sign of an invisible grace. Christ himself is the original sacrament: the visible face of the invisible God (Colossians 1:15). The Church is the sacrament of Christ: she is his body and the sign through which he acts. The seven sacraments are the Church’s acts.”", "Say: “The Church is one complex reality made of a human element and a divine element (LG 8). She has a visible structure: a Pope, bishops, parishes, buildings, laws. She is also a spiritual communion, the Body of Christ, a temple of the Spirit. These are not two churches; they are two aspects of one reality, like Jesus, who is one person with a human and divine nature. Gaudium et Spes calls her ‘a society of the faithful, and a mystical body.’ This is why scandals in the visible Church do not destroy her holiness: her holiness comes from Christ.”", "Say: “What follows? The Church is not a club or an NGO. She cannot be evaluated only by sociology or efficiency. Nor can we dismiss the visible structure as unimportant. The Church is where we meet Christ in the sacraments, Scripture and community. The Church is both ‘holy and always in need of purification’ (LG 8), always in need of reform. She is a sinful people with a holy Lord. Francis de Sales loved the Church’s sacraments and knew people’s weaknesses. The Church is also a communion: the Greek koinonia means sharing in the life of the Trinity and in the life of each other.”"],
    ask=["What is a sacrament? (A visible sign of invisible grace.)", "In what sense is the Church a sacrament? (A sign and instrument of union with God and of human unity.)", "How can the Church be holy and need reform? (Her holiness is Christ’s; her members are sinners.)"],
    practice=["Draw two columns: “Visible” and “Invisible” aspects of the Church.", "Fill in with at least five items each (Pope and bishops, buildings / grace, the Holy Spirit, communion of saints).", "Draw arrows connecting pairs.", "Write a paragraph: “Why the Church is not just a human organization.”"],
    close="Pray: “Lord, make your Church a sign of your love.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look for the invisible in a visible sign at Mass.”")),

'10-1-3': dict(
  saint="Ananias of Damascus: a disciple who greeted his enemy as “Brother Saul.”",
  tryit=["Match the biblical images with their meaning.", "Draw one.", "Say what each asks of members."],
  saintStory="Ananias lived in Damascus, and was one of the disciples. The Lord spoke to him in a vision: “Rise and go to the street called Straight, and at the house of Judas look for a man of Tarsus named Saul.” Ananias was horrified. “Lord, I have heard from many about this man, how much evil he has done to your saints at Jerusalem; and here he has authority from the chief priests to bind all who call upon your name.” The Lord said, “Go, for he is a chosen instrument of mine.”\nAnanias went, laid his hands on Saul, and said, “Brother Saul, the Lord Jesus who appeared to you on the road by which you came has sent me that you may regain your sight and be filled with the Holy Spirit.” Immediately something like scales fell from Saul’s eyes, and he was baptized.\nPaul’s first experience of the Church was Ananias’s greeting, “Brother.” The Lord had said on the road: “Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?” To strike the Church is to strike Christ.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a photograph of a family or team with many people in it. Say, “One photo, many faces. The Bible gives the Church three pictures like this, and each one shows something the others cannot.” Ask each person which picture of the Church they would draw first.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church is the People of God. God called Abraham and Israel as a people. In Christ, he calls all nations. 1 Peter 2:9-10: ‘You are a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, God’s own people… Once you were no people but now you are God’s people.’ The People of God have Christ as head, the Holy Spirit in their hearts, the law of love, and the Kingdom as their goal. Members are all baptized. The people share in Christ’s roles as priest, prophet and king.”", "Say: “The Body of Christ. Paul uses this image in 1 Corinthians 12 and Romans 12. ‘For just as the body is one and has many members… so it is with Christ.’ We are many members with different gifts but one body. The head is Christ (Ephesians 1:22-23; Colossians 1:18). When one member suffers, all suffer; and Christ identifies with the body: ‘Why do you persecute me?’ The Eucharist makes the Church: we become what we receive (Augustine). The Church’s unity is not uniformity; it requires every member.”", "Say: “The Temple of the Spirit. 1 Corinthians 3:16: ‘Do you not know that you are God’s temple and that God’s Spirit dwells in you?’ The Spirit is the soul of the Body, giving life, unity, gifts and holiness. The Spirit gives each member charisms for the good of all. Other images: the Bride of Christ (Ephesians 5), the Flock, the Vine, the Field, the Building, the Family of God. Each shows an aspect. Together they say that the Church is a communion, not just an organization. Our response: we belong to the body, we use our gifts, we care for the weaker members, and we honor the Spirit in each person.”"],
    ask=["Name three images of the Church. (People of God, Body of Christ, Temple of the Spirit.)", "Who is the head of the Body? (Christ.)", "What does the Spirit give members? (Life, unity, charisms.)"],
    practice=["Choose three images and draw them.", "Under each, write a Scripture verse and a one-sentence meaning.", "Write what each image asks of a member: belonging, gifts, care.", "Share your drawing with a partner and discuss which image speaks to you most."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, make us one body.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look for the gifts you can bring to your parish.”")),

'10-2-1': dict(
  saint="Frances Cabrini: a small, frail Italian sister who built hospitals and schools for immigrants.",
  tryit=["Explain how the Church is one and yet divided.", "List signs of holiness in the Church.", "Name saints of different states of life."],
  saintStory="Frances Cabrini was born in 1850 in Lombardy, Italy, the youngest of thirteen children. She was frail, and afraid of water after almost drowning. She wanted to be a missionary in China. After several rejections, she founded the Missionary Sisters of the Sacred Heart in 1880.\nShe asked Pope Leo XIII to send her to China, but he said, “Not to the East, but to the West.” In 1889, she crossed the Atlantic to New York, where Italian immigrants were poor and often neglected. She started schools, orphanages and hospitals, from New York to Chicago, New Orleans, Seattle and Latin America. Though afraid of water, she crossed the ocean more than thirty times.\nShe became a naturalized U.S. citizen in 1909, and died in Chicago in 1917. In 1946 she became the first American citizen to be canonized. Her life was holiness in action.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show the Nicene Creed line “one, holy, catholic and apostolic.” Ask, “Looking at the Church honestly, is she one? Is she holy? What would you point to?” Take honest answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The first mark is that the Church is one. Her unity comes from the Trinity: one Lord, one faith, one Baptism (Ephesians 4:5). The Church is one in her faith, her worship (the sacraments) and her government (apostolic succession, with the Pope as the visible center). But Christians are divided. The Catechism admits that ‘from the beginning, this one Church has been marked by a great variety’ (CCC 814), and that there have been wounds to unity: heresies, schisms. The Orthodox and Protestants have separated from full communion. Vatican II recognizes that ‘many elements of sanctification and of truth’ are found outside the visible boundaries of the Catholic Church. The Church works for ecumenism.”", "Say: “The second mark is that the Church is holy. Not because every member is, but because her source is the holy God, her means are holy (the Word and sacraments), and she is the place where the holiness of the saints blossoms. Vatican II taught the universal call to holiness: ‘All the faithful, whatever their condition or state, are called by the Lord… to that perfection of sanctity by which the Father himself is perfect.’ (LG 11) The Church is ‘at once holy and always in need of purification.’ Holiness is shown in the saints. Mother Cabrini, an immigrant sister; Gianna Molla, a mother and doctor; Pier Giorgio Frassati, a student; Josemaría Escrivá, a priest. The Church’s holiness is visible in her works: hospitals, schools, charity.”", "Say: “How do we hold unity and diversity together? Unity is not uniformity. The Church includes different rites, spiritualities and cultures. But unity requires communion: the same faith, sacraments and the bond with the bishops and the Pope. Holiness requires conversion: the Church kneels and confesses her sins in the liturgy: ‘I confess to almighty God and to you, my brothers and sisters.’ In 2000, John Paul II led the Church in a ‘purification of memory.’ The Church’s mission is to be a sign: ‘By this all men will know that you are my disciples, if you have love for one another.’ (John 13:35)”"],
    ask=["Where does the Church’s unity come from? (The Trinity.)", "What makes the Church holy? (Her holy source, means and the saints she produces.)", "What did Vatican II say about holiness? (All are called to it.)"],
    practice=["Make two lists: signs of unity and signs of division in the Church today.", "Make two more: signs of holiness and of sin.", "Identify five saints from different states of life: a student, a mother, a worker, a priest, an immigrant.", "Write a paragraph on how to respond when the Church disappoints you."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, make us one, as you and the Father are one.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for the unity of Christians.”")),

'10-2-2': dict(
  saint="Boniface: an English monk who cut down a sacred oak and carried the faith across Germany.",
  tryit=["Define each term: catholic, apostolic.", "Test them against a claim to be the Church.", "Explain the Pope’s role in each."],
  saintStory="Boniface was born around 675 in Wessex, England, and named Wynfrith. He became a Benedictine monk and a teacher. He felt called to missionary work among the Germanic peoples on the Continent. After a first attempt failed, he traveled to Rome, and in 719, Pope Gregory II sent him to Germany with a new name, Boniface, and a mission.\nIn about 723, at Geismar, he confronted the pagan cult of the god Donar (Thor) by taking an axe to a huge oak sacred to him. People waited for the god to strike him dead. When nothing happened, many were converted. He built a chapel with the wood. He founded monasteries and brought English nuns and monks to help. Boniface was made archbishop and always kept loyalty to the Pope.\nIn 754, in his late seventies, he went back to preach among the Frisians. On June 5, at Dokkum, a band of raiders attacked his party. According to tradition, he held up a book of the Gospels to protect himself and was killed. He is the “Apostle of Germany.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a map of Europe. Ask, “How many countries use the name ‘Catholic’ for their main church? What does ‘catholic’ mean? What does ‘apostolic’ mean?” Take guesses.",
    teach=["Say: “‘Catholic’ comes from the Greek katholikos, meaning ‘according to the whole.’ Ignatius of Antioch first used it around AD 107. The Church is catholic in two senses. First, because she has the fullness of the means of salvation: the whole faith, all the sacraments, and the apostolic ministry. Christ is present in her. Second, because she is sent to all people, in every age and place. Pentecost showed it: each heard in his own language. The Church is not tied to one culture; she adapts, as Boniface did: she built on what was good in the local culture and converted what was not. Catholic means universal, not uniform.”", "Say: “‘Apostolic’ has three senses (CCC 857). One: the Church was founded on the apostles; Ephesians 2:20 speaks of the household of God ‘built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Christ Jesus himself being the cornerstone.’ Two: she keeps and hands on the apostles’ teaching, the ‘deposit of faith.’ Three: she continues to be taught, sanctified and guided by the apostles’ successors, the college of bishops, assisted by the priests, together with the Pope, Peter’s successor. This is apostolic succession, going back by laying on of hands to the apostles. The word also means ‘sent’: the Church is apostolic because she is missionary.”", "Say: “Why does it matter? Anyone can start a church and call it ‘Christian.’ Apostolic succession, the Church believes, ensures that she teaches what the apostles taught, that the sacraments are valid, and that she is united. The Orthodox Churches also have valid apostolic succession; Catholic teaching sees them as true Churches though not in full communion with the Pope. Most Protestant communities do not claim this succession. The Catholic Church holds that the Church of Christ ‘subsists in’ the Catholic Church (LG 8) with the fullness of means, while recognizing that ‘many elements of sanctification and of truth’ are found outside. The Pope serves as the guarantor of unity.”"],
    ask=["What does catholic mean? (According to the whole; the fullness of faith for all peoples.)", "What are three senses of apostolic? (Founded on the apostles, keeping their teaching, guided by their successors.)", "What does the Pope guarantee? (Unity and fidelity.)"],
    practice=["Draw a chain of apostolic succession from Jesus to the apostles to your local bishop.", "Ask: How could you find your bishop’s lineage? (Many dioceses list it.)", "List three ways the Church is catholic in your parish (languages, cultures, ages).", "Write a short answer to a friend who says, ‘Any church can claim to be Christ’s.’"],
    close="Pray: “Lord, keep us faithful to the faith of the apostles.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look up the line of your bishop’s ordination.”")),

'10-2-3': dict(
  saint="Josemaría Escrivá: a priest who taught that ordinary work can be holy.",
  tryit=["Fill in who does what in the Church.", "Name the laity’s role in the world.", "Write one way to sanctify your work."],
  saintStory="Josemaría Escrivá was born in 1902 in Barbastro, Spain. After his father’s business failed and the family fell into poverty, he decided to become a priest, and was ordained in 1925. On October 2, 1928, during a retreat, he said he “saw” what God wanted: a way for ordinary people to pursue holiness in their daily work. He founded Opus Dei, “Work of God.”\nHe taught that all baptized people are called to holiness, and that work, family life and friendships can all be paths to God. “There is something holy, something divine, hidden in the most ordinary situations, and it is up to each one of you to discover it.” The members were lay people, professionals, students and workers who stayed in their jobs.\nThe Spanish Civil War forced him into hiding. Opus Dei grew to tens of thousands of members in many countries. He died in 1975 and was canonized in 2002. Vatican II, in teaching the universal call to holiness, echoed his message.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If I said ‘Church leader,’ whom would you picture? Now picture a Church member who is a nurse, an engineer or a student. Is one more ‘in the Church’ than the other?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church is structured. Vatican II describes the People of God as having ordained ministers and lay faithful, all equal in dignity by Baptism but with different roles. The hierarchy is composed of bishops, priests and deacons. The Pope is the bishop of Rome and the successor of Peter, with supreme authority in the Church. Bishops are successors of the apostles, who lead dioceses and together form the College of Bishops, with the Pope as head. Priests assist the bishop, preaching, celebrating the sacraments, and leading parishes. Deacons serve in word, liturgy and charity. The word ‘hierarchy’ means ‘sacred rule,’ but Jesus said the leader must be a servant (Mark 10:42-45).”", "Say: “The laity are all the baptized who are not ordained or in the consecrated state. By Baptism, they share in Christ’s priestly, prophetic and royal mission. Their special field is the world: family, work, culture, politics, economics, the media. ‘The laity, by their very vocation, seek the kingdom of God by engaging in temporal affairs and directing them according to the plan of God.’ (LG 31) The laity also have roles within the Church: readers, catechists, parish councils, Eucharistic ministers, teachers, theologians, pastoral associates. Lay people are about 99.9 percent of the Church. The mission of the Church to the world is carried mostly by the laity.”", "Say: “The consecrated life includes religious (monks, nuns, friars, sisters) who profess vows of poverty, chastity and obedience, as well as consecrated virgins, hermits and members of secular institutes. They aren’t a third class between clergy and laity but a sign of the Kingdom. Different roles do not mean different value. The Church is communion: the hierarchy serves the people, and the people support the hierarchy. A common error is clericalism, thinking that only the clergy ‘are the Church.’ Pope Francis has repeatedly warned against it. The opposite error would be to disregard the authority Christ gave to the apostles. St. Josemaría’s message: your work is a path to holiness.”"],
    ask=["Name the three degrees of Holy Orders. (Bishops, priests, deacons.)", "What is the laity’s special field? (The world: family, work, culture.)", "What is clericalism? (Treating the clergy as the only real Church.)"],
    practice=["Draw a chart showing the roles in the Church: Pope, bishops, priests, deacons, religious, laity.", "For each, write one task and one Scripture reference.", "Under the laity, write five ordinary jobs and how each could be a path to holiness.", "Write a one-sentence offering of your own work or studies."],
    close="Pray the Morning Offering. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Offer your next piece of homework to God as prayer.”")),

'10-3-1': dict(
  saint="Cyprian of Carthage: a bishop who held the Church together through persecution and plague.",
  tryit=["Make a timeline of the persecutions.", "Mark how the Church responded to the lapsed.", "Write what the Church learned."],
  saintStory="Cyprian was a wealthy lawyer and teacher of rhetoric in Carthage, North Africa. He became a Christian around 246, gave away much of his wealth, and within two years was made bishop of Carthage. In 250, Emperor Decius ordered all citizens to sacrifice to the gods. Many Christians gave in. Cyprian went into hiding and directed his flock by letters, a decision for which he was criticized.\nAfter the persecution, he faced a crisis: what to do with the lapsed, Christians who had sacrificed or bought certificates saying they had? Some confessors wanted instant forgiveness; some wanted to ban them forever. Cyprian chose a middle way: they must do penance and then could be restored. In 252, a plague hit Carthage. Pagans abandoned the sick. Cyprian urged Christians to care for everyone, including their enemies.\nHe wrote On the Unity of the Church: “He cannot have God for his Father who has not the Church for his mother.” In 258, in a new persecution, he refused to sacrifice and was beheaded.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write two dates on the board, AD 30 and AD 313. Say, “In between, a handful of frightened fishermen became a movement the Roman Empire could not stop. What do you think it took?” Collect guesses and keep them for the end of the lesson.",
    teach=["Say: “From Pentecost to Constantine is almost 300 years. The early Church began in Jerusalem, a Jewish community, and spread through the Roman Empire along roads and sea lanes. Paul’s missionary journeys took the Gospel to Asia Minor and Greece, and then to Rome. By AD 100, there were Christians in most major cities. By 300, perhaps 5 to 10 percent of the empire. The Church grew by personal witness: the way Christians lived, cared for the poor and sick, treated women, refused to abandon infants (as pagans did), and faced death.”", "Say: “Persecution was sporadic and local until the third century. Nero blamed Christians for the fire in Rome in AD 64. Domitian’s time. Trajan told Pliny: Christians must not be sought out, but punished if denounced. Decius in 250 began the first empire-wide persecution. Valerian in 257. And Diocletian’s Great Persecution, 303-311, was the worst: churches burned, Scriptures destroyed, clergy arrested. In response, the Church developed her organization. Bishops led dioceses, synods met, a canon of Scripture took shape, and the Eucharistic liturgy was settled. The writers called Apologists defended Christians. Tertullian: ‘The blood of the martyrs is the seed of the Church.’”", "Say: “In 313, Constantine and Licinius issued the Edict of Milan. Constantine favored the Church, built basilicas, and called the Council of Nicaea in 325. Theodosius made Christianity the empire’s religion in 380. The change brought opportunity and danger: the Church was no longer persecuted, but could be politically entangled, and nominal membership grew. Monasticism arose partly as a protest: those who wanted a radical life went to the desert. A lesson from Cyprian: in crisis, the Church must hold truth and mercy together, neither compromising the faith nor driving away the weak. The early Church shows what a faithful minority can do.”"],
    ask=["Name a persecution. (Nero, Decius, Diocletian.)", "What did Cyprian decide about the lapsed? (Penance, then restoration.)", "What did Constantine’s Edict of Milan do? (Granted freedom of worship.)"],
    practice=["Make a timeline from AD 30 to 380 with ten events: Pentecost, Paul’s journeys, Nero, destruction of Jerusalem (70), Ignatius (107), Decius (250), Diocletian (303), Milan (313), Nicaea (325), Theodosius (380).", "Mark persecutions in red and growth in green.", "Write what the Church learned from the lapsed.", "Compare with what pressures Christians face today."],
    close="Pray for persecuted Christians today. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for the courage to stand for your faith.”")),

'10-3-2': dict(
  saint="Hilary of Poitiers: a bishop who was exiled for defending the Trinity.",
  tryit=["Trace how the Creed grew.", "Match each heresy with its council.", "Explain each line of the Creed it defends."],
  saintStory="Hilary was born around 310 in Gaul, to a pagan family. A scholar, he read the Scriptures in search of the truth about God, and was struck by John 1:1, “In the beginning was the Word.” He was baptized in his thirties, was married with a daughter, and around 350 was elected bishop of Poitiers.\nEmperor Constantius II supported the Arians, and pressured the Western bishops to condemn Athanasius. Hilary refused. In 356 he was exiled to Phrygia, in Asia Minor, for about four years. There he studied Eastern theology and wrote On the Trinity, a major work defending the divinity of the Son. He also wrote commentaries on Matthew and the Psalms. He was allowed back, and returned to Gaul to work for peace.\nHe died in 367. He was called the “Athanasius of the West” and is a Doctor of the Church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write the first line of the Nicene Creed on the board. Ask, “Why do we say the Creed every Sunday? Who wrote it? Why so many words?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The first Christian creeds were short baptismal professions: ‘Jesus is Lord’ (Romans 10:9), then the questions in three parts: ‘Do you believe in God the Father… in Jesus Christ… in the Holy Spirit?’ That became the Apostles’ Creed, an old Roman baptismal creed. As heresies arose, the Church needed more precise language. The ecumenical councils produced the Nicene-Constantinopolitan Creed, which we pray at Mass. Councils are gatherings of bishops from around the world, called to settle questions of faith. The Church recognizes 21 ecumenical councils, from Nicaea I (325) to Vatican II (1962-65).”", "Say: “The first four councils defined the Trinity and Christ. Nicaea I (325): the Son is ‘consubstantial’ (homoousios) with the Father, against Arius. Constantinople I (381): the Holy Spirit is God, ‘who with the Father and the Son is adored and glorified,’ against those who denied the Spirit’s divinity. Ephesus (431): Mary is Theotokos, against Nestorius; Jesus is one Person. Chalcedon (451): Jesus has two natures, divine and human, in one Person, against Monophysitism. Later, Constantinople II (553) and III (680-81) completed the picture, with two wills in Christ. Nicaea II (787) approved the veneration of icons, against iconoclasm.”", "Say: “Why was this not just hair-splitting? Each word protects the Gospel. If the Son is not truly God, then Jesus cannot save us. If he’s not truly man, he cannot represent us. The word ‘homoousios’ was not in the Bible, but it expressed what the Bible says. Hilary and Athanasius saw that the whole of Christian life depends on it. The Creed is also a prayer, and the Church’s summary of the faith. It is not an opinion poll but a gift received. The ‘filioque’ clause (the Spirit proceeds from the Father ‘and the Son’) was added in the West and is still a difference with the Orthodox. Councils also settled calendar questions (Easter date) and church order.”"],
    ask=["What did Nicaea declare? (The Son is consubstantial with the Father.)", "What did Chalcedon teach? (Christ has two natures in one Person.)", "Why do councils matter? (They protect the faith.)"],
    practice=["Make a table of the first four councils: year, heresy, teaching, line in the Creed.", "Underline the key word (homoousios, Theotokos, two natures).", "Write each in simple English.", "Pray the Creed slowly, noticing each line."],
    close="Pray the Nicene Creed aloud together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray the Creed at Mass, thinking of Hilary.”")),

'10-3-3': dict(
  saint="Isidore of Seville: a bishop who gathered the learning of the ancient world into one book.",
  tryit=["Read a short excerpt from a Father.", "Say what question it answers.", "Rate its usefulness today."],
  saintStory="Isidore was born around 560 in Spain, into a family of saints: his brothers Leander and Fulgentius and his sister Florentina were all honored as saints. After the Visigoths conquered Spain, Leander, the archbishop of Seville, raised Isidore, and when Leander died, Isidore succeeded him around 600. He served for over thirty years.\nIsidore was concerned that with the collapse of the Roman Empire, ancient learning would be lost. He wrote the Etymologies, a twenty-book encyclopedia of everything: grammar, medicine, law, agriculture, the heavens, war, ships, animals, buildings, and the Church. He explained things by tracing the origin of words. It became the standard reference for the Middle Ages, copied in hundreds of manuscripts. He also presided at the Fourth Council of Toledo (633) which required every cathedral to set up a school.\nHe died in 636. He is a Doctor of the Church, and has been proposed as the patron of the internet.",
  script=dict(
    open="Read aloud a short, striking line from a Church Father, such as Augustine’s “You have made us for yourself, O Lord, and our heart is restless until it rests in you,” without saying who wrote it. Have students guess the century and the author, then reveal.",
    teach=["Say: “The Fathers of the Church are Christian writers of the first centuries whose teaching is a witness to the apostolic faith. Four marks: orthodoxy of doctrine, holiness of life, Church approval, and antiquity. Different groups: the Apostolic Fathers (Clement, Ignatius, Polycarp, the Didache); the Apologists (Justin, Tertullian); the Greek Fathers (Athanasius, Basil, the two Gregorys, Chrysostom, Cyril of Alexandria); the Latin Fathers (Ambrose, Jerome, Augustine, Gregory the Great, Isidore). Eastern Fathers wrote on the Trinity and spiritual life; Western Fathers on grace, the Church and law.”", "Say: “What they gave. They interpreted the Scriptures, especially through the spiritual senses. They defended the faith against heresies. They developed theological language. They gave catechesis to converts and preached on the sacraments. They also modeled holiness. Their testimony to the liturgy is valuable. Justin describes the Mass in AD 155. The Didache (around AD 100) describes Baptism, fasting and the Eucharist. Ignatius and Irenaeus defend the Real Presence. Cyprian and Augustine write of the Church. Without the Fathers, we would not have the Creed or the canon.”", "Say: “Weigh them carefully. The Fathers are not infallible individually; they sometimes disagreed or erred on secondary matters. Origen’s speculations were condemned; Augustine’s view of limbo was a theory. What matters is their moral unanimity on matters of faith (consensus patrum). Vatican II says their writings ‘testify to the living presence of this Tradition.’ (DV 8) Protestants also value the Fathers, though differ in interpretation. The Liturgy of the Hours includes readings from them each day. Reading the Fathers helps us see that the Catholic faith is ancient. Some good starting points: Augustine’s Confessions, Athanasius’s On the Incarnation, Chrysostom’s homilies, and Gregory’s Pastoral Rule.”"],
    ask=["Who are the Fathers? (Early writers whose teaching witnesses to the apostolic faith.)", "Name a Greek Father and a Latin Father. (Athanasius; Augustine.)", "What does the consensus of the Fathers show? (What the Church believed from the beginning.)"],
    practice=["Give each pair a short Father excerpt (Ignatius on the Eucharist, Justin on the Mass, Irenaeus on Mary, Augustine on grace).", "Write: what question does this answer, and what does it show about early belief?", "Rate how useful this is for a modern Catholic.", "Share findings."],
    close="Pray a collect of the Father you read. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read a Father’s homily in the Office of Readings.”")),

'10-4-1': dict(
  saint="Patrick: a former slave who returned to convert his captors.",
  tryit=["Trace the spread of the faith on a map.", "Mark the monasteries.", "Say what the monks preserved."],
  saintStory="Patrick was born around 385 in Roman Britain, the son of a deacon and grandson of a priest. At sixteen, he was kidnapped by Irish raiders and taken to Ireland as a slave, where he tended sheep for six years. He had not taken faith seriously before, but alone on the hills, he began to pray, “a hundred times a day and almost as many at night.”\nAfter six years, he heard a voice in a dream telling him that his ship was ready. He escaped, traveled two hundred miles to the coast and found passage to Britain. He later had a dream in which a man named Victoricus gave him a letter: “The Voice of the Irish.” In it, people from Ireland were calling, “Holy boy, come and walk among us again.”\nHe studied for the priesthood, was ordained a bishop and returned to Ireland around 432. He baptized thousands, and trained clergy and founded churches. He wrote the Confession, a humble account of his life. He is said to have used the shamrock to teach the Trinity. He died around 461.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “How would you feel about returning to a country where you had been enslaved, to help the people who enslaved you? What would make you do it?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “After Rome’s fall in 476, Europe was fragmented. Monasteries became the centers of civilization. Benedict’s Rule (around 530) shaped Western monasticism with ‘pray and work.’ Irish monasteries, following Patrick, became centers of learning and mission: Columba founded Iona (563), Columbanus went to the Continent and founded Luxeuil and Bobbio. Anglo-Saxon missionaries: Augustine of Canterbury (597), Aidan, Bede, Boniface in Germany. Cyril and Methodius to the Slavs. By about 1000, nearly all Europe was Christian. Monks copied manuscripts, preserving the Bible, the Fathers and classical works. They farmed, taught and welcomed travelers.”", "Say: “The Church and the new rulers. Clovis, king of the Franks, was baptized around 496. Charlemagne was crowned emperor by Pope Leo III on Christmas Day, 800. This produced a close relationship of Church and state, with benefits and problems. Bishops became feudal lords. Kings appointed bishops, known as lay investiture. Pope Gregory VII (1073-85) led a reform movement, insisting the Church be free from lay control and clergy be celibate. The conflict with Emperor Henry IV culminated in his penance at Canossa in 1077. The monastic reform movements, such as Cluny, the Cistercians and Carthusians, renewed the Church.”", "Say: “The high Middle Ages, 1100-1300, were the Church’s great period of building and learning. The mendicant orders (Franciscans, Dominicans) preached in cities. Universities rose. Scholastic theology blossomed with Anselm, Abelard, Bonaventure and Aquinas. Gothic cathedrals rose. There were also darker features: the Crusades, the Inquisition, anti-Jewish violence, and corruption in some of the clergy and papacy. The popes moved to Avignon (1309-1377) and a Western Schism (1378-1417) produced rival popes. These abuses prepared the ground for the Reformation. A fair view is that the medieval Church was a mixture of holiness and corruption, but the faith sank deep roots in European culture.”"],
    ask=["Who evangelized Ireland? (Patrick.)", "Who was crowned emperor in 800? (Charlemagne.)", "What was the Western Schism? (A period when rival claimants to the papacy divided the Church.)"],
    practice=["On a map of Europe, mark key monasteries (Monte Cassino, Iona, Luxeuil, Cluny, Clairvaux), the baptism of Clovis, and Charlemagne’s coronation.", "Draw arrows for the missions.", "Write a list of what the monks preserved.", "Compare the strengths and weaknesses of the Church in the Middle Ages."],
    close="Pray the Breastplate of Saint Patrick or: “Christ with me, Christ before me.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look up Patrick’s Confession.”")),

'10-4-2': dict(
  saint="John Fisher: the only bishop in England who refused to bow to Henry VIII.",
  tryit=["Sort the causes of the Reformation.", "Mark which were abuses and which were doctrines.", "Write what the Church lost and gained."],
  saintStory="John Fisher was born in 1469 in Yorkshire. He became bishop of Rochester in 1504, a small poor diocese, which he served for over thirty years and refused to leave for richer ones. He was one of the most learned men in England, a patron of scholars like Erasmus, and a chancellor of Cambridge University. He lived simply and was devoted to his flock. He preached against Luther’s teaching, and also called for reform within the Church.\nWhen Henry VIII sought to annul his marriage to Catherine of Aragon, Fisher defended the marriage in court, saying he would die for it. In 1534, Parliament passed the Act of Supremacy, making the king head of the Church of England. Fisher was the only bishop to refuse the oath. He was imprisoned in the Tower of London. In 1535, the pope made him a cardinal, and an angry Henry said he would send the head to Rome without a hat to put it on.\nFisher was beheaded on June 22, 1535, at sixty-six. Thomas More followed him two weeks later.",
  script=dict(
    open="Draw a long crack down the middle of the board. Say, “Christianity has been split in 1054 and again in the 1500s. Write one thing you already know or have heard about each split.” Let students add notes along the crack.",
    teach=["Say: “Medieval schisms: the East-West Schism of 1054 split Rome from the Eastern Churches; the Western Schism of 1378-1417 had two and then three claimants to the papacy. In the late Middle Ages, the Church was weakened by corruption: bishops who held many offices and didn’t reside in their dioceses, poorly trained clergy, selling of indulgences, and fighting among popes and princes. Reformers like John Wycliffe in England and Jan Hus in Bohemia had called for change, and Hus was burned. There were saints, too: Catherine of Siena, Bernardino, Vincent Ferrer. The Renaissance popes spent lavishly on art and war. Gutenberg’s printing press (1450s) spread ideas quickly. Nationalism and kings wanting power also mattered.”", "Say: “Luther’s protest in 1517 started a spiral. By 1521 he was excommunicated. In Switzerland, Zwingli and Calvin rejected much of Catholic worship and sacramental teaching. The Peace of Augsburg (1555) let each prince choose Lutheran or Catholic faith for his realm. In England, Henry VIII separated from Rome in 1534 for political and personal reasons, but doctrine changed under Edward VI and Elizabeth I. Wars of religion devastated Europe, such as the Thirty Years’ War (1618-48). The Reformers raised legitimate questions: Scripture, grace, abuses. But the Catholic Church holds that they went too far in rejecting the sacraments, the priesthood, the papacy and Tradition.”", "Say: “Different views on the split. Protestants speak of the recovery of the Gospel. Catholics speak of a tragic rupture. Today many scholars see it as both. What happened afterward: the Catholic Reformation (Trent, Jesuits, saints) renewed the Church; Protestant churches multiplied. Today, about 2.3 billion Christians: 1.4 billion Catholic, about 1 billion Protestant and Anglican, 260 million Orthodox. The 500th anniversary in 2017 was marked by common prayer; Pope Francis traveled to Sweden to join Lutherans. We can acknowledge that both sides sinned. The Catechism: ‘The Church’s ecumenical task…’ requires repentance and prayer for unity.”"],
    ask=["What caused the Reformation? (Abuses, Luther’s questions, printing, politics.)", "Who refused the Act of Supremacy among the bishops? (John Fisher.)", "What was the Peace of Augsburg? (Each prince chose Lutheran or Catholic faith for his realm.)"],
    practice=["Make a table: Cause, Type (abuse, doctrine, politics), Catholic response.", "Include at least eight items.", "Draw a line to separate things the Church admitted were wrong from things she defended.", "Write a paragraph beginning, “If I had lived in 1517, I would have…”"],
    close="Pray for the unity of all Christians. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Learn the difference between Catholic and Lutheran beliefs.”")),

'10-4-3': dict(
  saint="Paul VI: the pope who steered the Council to its end and wrote Evangelii Nuntiandi.",
  tryit=["Match each document of Vatican II with its message.", "Mark a change at your parish.", "Write what the Council still asks."],
  saintStory="Giovanni Battista Montini was born in 1897 in northern Italy and became a Vatican official, then archbishop of Milan, where he reached out to workers. He was elected pope in 1963 after John XXIII died, and took the name Paul. He was a thoughtful man, shy, who had a heavy responsibility: to lead Vatican II to its conclusion. He did so in 1965.\nHe was the first pope to travel by plane, going to the Holy Land in 1964, where he embraced the Orthodox patriarch Athenagoras, and to the United Nations in 1965, where he cried “No more war!” In December 1965, he and Athenagoras lifted the mutual excommunications of 1054. He reformed the Mass, set up the Synod of Bishops and the internationalization of the Curia.\nHe was criticized from all sides. His 1968 encyclical Humanae Vitae was widely rejected. He wrote in 1975 Evangelii Nuntiandi, a call to evangelization. He died in 1978 and was canonized in 2018.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show two pictures, a church interior from 1955 and one from today. Say, “Spot the differences, then spot what has not changed.” Give a minute of silent looking, then collect observations in two columns.",
    teach=["Say: “Vatican II (1962-65) was the 21st ecumenical council. It began under John XXIII and ended under Paul VI. It produced four constitutions, nine decrees and three declarations. The main documents. Lumen Gentium: the Church as mystery, People of God, the hierarchy, the laity, the call to holiness, Mary. Dei Verbum: Revelation, Scripture and Tradition; encouragement to read the Bible. Sacrosanctum Concilium: reform of the liturgy, with ‘full, conscious and active participation’; the vernacular. Gaudium et Spes: the Church in the modern world, human dignity, marriage, culture, economics and peace. Also Nostra Aetate (other religions, rejecting antisemitism), Unitatis Redintegratio (ecumenism), Dignitatis Humanae (religious freedom).”", "Say: “What changed? The Mass in local languages, the priest facing the people (not required, but common), greater use of Scripture, lay readers and ministers, a new Rite of Christian Initiation of Adults, the permanent diaconate was restored, ecumenical dialogue began, lay apostolate was encouraged. What did not change: doctrine, the Creed, the sacraments, the priesthood, the papacy, moral teaching. Pope Benedict XVI said there are two ‘hermeneutics’ or ways of reading the Council: a ‘hermeneutic of discontinuity and rupture,’ which sees the Council as a break with the past, and a ‘hermeneutic of reform,’ in which the Church renews herself while remaining the same subject. He affirmed the latter.”", "Say: “The post-conciliar period was turbulent. Many left the priesthood and religious life in the 1960s-70s. Mass attendance fell in the West. Some believed the Council changed too much; some thought it had not gone far enough. The Council’s call to holiness, the Bible, and mission is still being implemented. Pope John Paul II called the Council ‘the great grace of the twentieth century.’ The Catechism of the Catholic Church (1992) was the council’s fruit. Today, a key question: what does it mean to be a Church in dialogue with the world, faithful to Christ, confident in the Gospel? Paul VI’s Evangelii Nuntiandi gave one answer: the Church exists to evangelize.”"],
    ask=["Who closed Vatican II? (Paul VI.)", "Name two changes after Vatican II. (Vernacular Mass; greater lay participation; ecumenism.)", "What does a ‘hermeneutic of reform’ mean? (The Church renews while remaining the same.)"],
    practice=["Make a table of the four constitutions: main idea, one change, one thing to learn more about.", "Add Nostra Aetate and Dignitatis Humanae.", "Look up one paragraph of Lumen Gentium or Gaudium et Spes online and summarize it.", "Write what you think the Council still asks of the Church."],
    close="Pray the Prayer for the Council or the Veni Creator. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read the opening of Gaudium et Spes.”")),

'10-5-1': dict(
  saint="Paul Miki: a Japanese Jesuit who preached from the cross.",
  tryit=["Plan an invitation, not an argument.", "Write your two-minute story.", "Rehearse with a partner."],
  saintStory="Paul Miki was born around 1562 in Japan, to a wealthy family. He was educated by Jesuits and became a Jesuit brother, a gifted preacher who brought many to the faith. Christianity had come to Japan with Francis Xavier in 1549 and had grown to hundreds of thousands. But in 1587 the ruler Hideyoshi began to fear the foreigners and ordered missionaries to leave.\nIn December 1596, he ordered the arrest of Christians in Kyoto and Osaka. Twenty-six were seized: six Franciscans, three Japanese Jesuits including Paul Miki, and seventeen lay Japanese, including three boys who were altar servers. They were marched about 600 miles through winter to Nagasaki. On February 5, 1597, they were crucified on a hill overlooking the city.\nPaul Miki preached from his cross. “I am a Japanese by birth and a Brother of the Society of Jesus. I have committed no crime and the only reason why I am condemned to die is that I have been teaching the doctrine of our Lord Jesus Christ. I am very happy to die for such a reason.” He forgave his persecutors. After the martyrdoms, the Church in Japan survived underground for over 250 years.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Think about a time someone tried to convince you of something. What worked? What made you shut down?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church’s mission, from Jesus’ command in Matthew 28:19, is to evangelize. Paul VI wrote in Evangelii Nuntiandi: ‘Evangelizing is the grace and vocation proper to the Church, her deepest identity.’ Pope John Paul II called for a ‘new evangelization,’ new in ardor, methods and expression, to reach those who have drifted from the faith. Pope Francis in Evangelii Gaudium (2013) insists that the Gospel is joy and that every Christian is a missionary disciple. Evangelization has several aspects: witness of life, proclamation, conversion, entering the community, receiving sacraments and apostolate.”", "Say: “Why do we do it? Because Christ commanded it. Because we want to share the gift of faith. Because God wants all to be saved (1 Timothy 2:4). And because we believe the Gospel is true and the best news. How? The Church distinguishes among proclamation (the kerygma, the basic announcement that Jesus died and rose to save us), catechesis (teaching the faith), and apologetics (reasoned defense). The first message is: God loves you, Jesus died and rose for you, and he calls you to follow. Mission isn’t coercion: the Church respects freedom. Dignitatis Humanae: ‘The truth cannot impose itself except by the force of its own truth.’”", "Say: “Evangelization involves inculturation: bringing the Gospel into the culture and letting it be expressed in local forms, as Ricci did in China and Cyril and Methodius among the Slavs. It also includes service of the poor and justice, as Jesus did. Today, mission fields are everywhere, including our neighborhoods. About a quarter of those raised Catholic in the U.S. have left the Church. Many are ‘nones’ with no religion. Good evangelization starts with listening, friendship and witness; it asks questions and invites; it avoids being pushy or argumentative. 1 Peter 3:15 says to be ready to give an account with gentleness and reverence. Francis said, ‘The Church grows not by proselytizing, but by attraction.’”"],
    ask=["What is the new evangelization? (Evangelization new in ardor, methods and expression, for those who have drifted.)", "What is the kerygma? (The basic announcement of Jesus’ death and resurrection.)", "What is inculturation? (Expressing the Gospel in local culture.)"],
    practice=["Write a two-minute story: one moment when your faith mattered.", "Practice sharing it with a partner without arguing or pressuring.", "Write three questions you would ask a friend who is not religious.", "Plan one concrete invitation: Mass, a youth group, a service project."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, send me to those who need to hear your name.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Invite one person to something at your parish.”")),

'10-5-2': dict(
  saint="John Paul II: the pope who gathered the world’s religions to pray for peace at Assisi.",
  tryit=["Match each approach to a church teaching.", "Name what we share with each religion.", "Write what we cannot give up."],
  saintStory="Karol Wojtyła was born in 1920 in Wadowice, Poland. In his youth he loved theater, poetry and sport. During the German occupation, he worked in a quarry and chemical plant and secretly studied for the priesthood. He was ordained in 1946, became a professor and then archbishop of Kraków. In 1978 he was elected pope, the first non-Italian in 455 years.\nHe traveled to more than 120 countries. He was the first pope to visit a synagogue, in Rome in 1986, where he called Jews “our elder brothers.” In 2000, he prayed at the Western Wall in Jerusalem and left a note asking forgiveness. On October 27, 1986, in Assisi, he invited leaders of the world’s religions to pray for peace, each in their own way; this is sometimes misunderstood as syncretism, but he insisted that they came together to pray, not to pray together as one.\nHe wrote the encyclical Ut Unum Sint (1995) on Christian unity. John Paul II died in 2005 and was canonized in 2014.",
  script=dict(
    open="Pose a scenario: a classmate of another faith invites you to a major religious feast. Do you go? What do you say? What do you not say? Let two volunteers act it out for two minutes before the class discusses.",
    teach=["Say: “Nostra Aetate (1965) is Vatican II’s declaration on non-Christian religions. It says: ‘The Catholic Church rejects nothing that is true and holy in these religions.’ It looks at what we share. Judaism: our roots; God’s covenant with Israel has not been revoked; Jesus, Mary and the apostles were Jews; the Church condemns antisemitism. Islam: Muslims worship the one God, honor Jesus as a prophet, and Mary; the Council urges mutual understanding. Hinduism and Buddhism: they seek truth about the human condition and ways of liberation. Nostra Aetate also says the Church ‘proclaims, and ever must proclaim Christ, the way, the truth, and the life.’”", "Say: “Ecumenism is the movement for unity among Christians. Unitatis Redintegratio (1964) says that all who are baptized are brothers and sisters in Christ, and that the Catholic Church is committed to restoring unity. We approach it by prayer, dialogue, shared service and study. Orthodox: close in sacraments and bishops; main differences are the Pope and the filioque. Protestants: share Scripture, Baptism and faith in Christ; differ on the Church’s authority, sacraments, and Mary. Ecumenism does not mean compromising doctrine, nor does it mean that all churches are equal. It means seeking the truth in charity. John Paul II said the Church must ‘breathe with both lungs.’”", "Say: “Interreligious dialogue has four forms: the dialogue of life (living together), of action (working together for justice), of theological exchange, and of religious experience (sharing prayer and spirituality). Questions arise. Doesn’t this relativize Christ? No. The Church holds that Jesus is the unique Savior of all (Acts 4:12), and that the fullness of truth is found in Him. But she respects the dignity and freedom of each person. Pope Benedict XVI’s Regensburg lecture (2006) raised the matter of faith and violence. Today the Church works with Jewish and Muslim groups on charity and peace. The ‘Document on Human Fraternity’ (2019), signed by Pope Francis and the Grand Imam of al-Azhar, is an example.”"],
    ask=["What does Nostra Aetate say? (The Church rejects nothing true and holy in other religions.)", "What is ecumenism? (The movement for Christian unity.)", "Does dialogue mean all religions are equal? (No; the Church holds Christ is the unique Savior.)"],
    practice=["Make a table: Judaism, Islam, Hinduism, Buddhism, Orthodoxy, Protestantism. In each: what we share; what we differ on.", "Add: one way Catholics can respectfully engage.", "Write two sentences on what you must not give up in dialogue.", "Plan one respectful question you could ask a friend of another faith."],
    close="Pray the Assisi prayer for peace or the Prayer of Saint Francis. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask a friend of another faith what they love about it.”")),

'10-5-3': dict(
  saint="Cornelius the centurion: a God-fearing Roman soldier whom Peter baptized.",
  tryit=["Sort teachings on salvation: what the Church holds firmly.", "Mark what the Church leaves to God.", "Write a careful reply to a friend."],
  saintStory="Cornelius was a centurion in the Roman army, stationed at Caesarea, in the Italian Cohort. He was a devout man who feared God, gave alms generously and prayed constantly. One afternoon, an angel appeared and said, “Your prayers and your alms have ascended as a memorial before God. Send men to Joppa for Simon, who is called Peter.”\nMeanwhile, Peter had a vision of a sheet descending with all kinds of animals, and a voice said, “Rise, Peter; kill and eat.” He objected, as many were unclean by Jewish law. The voice said, “What God has cleansed, you must not call common.” When Cornelius’s messengers arrived, Peter understood. He went to Cornelius’s house, though entering a Gentile home was forbidden for Jews. He said, “Truly I perceive that God shows no partiality, but in every nation any one who fears him and does what is right is acceptable to him.”\nWhile Peter was preaching, the Holy Spirit fell on all who heard, and he baptized them. Cornelius was the first Gentile convert in Acts.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write the Latin phrase on the board: “Extra ecclesiam nulla salus”, and ask, “What do you think it means? Who might it condemn? Who might it include?” Take honest answers. “It is one of the most misunderstood phrases.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church teaches that Christ is the only Savior, and that the Church is necessary for salvation. ‘There is salvation in no one else, for there is no other name under heaven given among men by which we must be saved.’ (Acts 4:12) ‘Outside the Church there is no salvation’ goes back to Origen and Cyprian. The Catechism explains: ‘Basing itself on Scripture and Tradition, the Council teaches that the Church, a pilgrim now on earth, is necessary for salvation: the one Christ is the mediator and the way of salvation; he is present to us in his body which is the Church.’ (CCC 846) All salvation comes from Christ the Head through the Church, his Body.”", "Say: “Yet it does not mean that only Catholics can be saved. The Council teaches, ‘Those who, through no fault of their own, do not know the Gospel of Christ or his Church, but nevertheless seek God with a sincere heart, and, moved by grace, try in their actions to do his will as they know it through the dictates of their conscience—those too may achieve eternal salvation.’ (LG 16) Cornelius was such a person. Those who know that the Catholic Church was founded by Christ as necessary, and yet refuse to enter or remain in her, cannot be saved. Baptized non-Catholics are in imperfect communion. God’s grace works beyond the visible boundaries. Baptism of desire and baptism of blood are recognized.”", "Say: “This leaves us two tasks. One, to proclaim Christ; the Church has an urgent mission since she has the fullness of the means of salvation. ‘Woe to me if I do not preach the Gospel.’ (1 Corinthians 9:16) Two, to trust God’s mercy, who ‘desires all men to be saved and to come to the knowledge of the truth.’ (1 Timothy 2:4) We do not judge the fate of any individual. We don’t know how many are saved. The Church has never declared that any particular person is in hell, not even Judas. And what about children who die unbaptized? The Church entrusts them to God’s mercy (CCC 1261); the International Theological Commission in 2007 gave reasons for hope. The Church’s teaching is balanced between universal salvific will and the necessity of Christ and the Church.”"],
    ask=["Is the Church necessary for salvation? (Yes; Christ saves through his Body, the Church.)", "Can non-Catholics be saved? (Yes, if they sincerely seek God and live by conscience, through no fault of their own.)", "Has the Church said anyone is in hell? (No.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart: What the Church teaches firmly / What the Church leaves to God’s mercy.", "Fill in with items such as: Christ is the only Savior; the Church is necessary; sincere seekers can be saved; baptism of desire; unbaptized infants.", "Write a careful reply to a friend who says, ‘So you think everyone else is going to hell?’", "Practice saying it kindly."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, you desire all to be saved. Bring everyone to you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for someone far from God by name.”")),

'10-6-1': dict(
  saint="Peter: the one for whom Jesus prayed that his faith would not fail.",
  tryit=["Sort statements into infallible and not.", "Cite the condition for each.", "Explain what infallibility is not."],
  saintStory="At the Last Supper, as the disciples argued about who was the greatest, Jesus turned to Simon: “Simon, Simon, behold, Satan demanded to have you, that he might sift you all like wheat, but I have prayed for you that your faith may not fail; and when you have turned again, strengthen your brethren.” Peter protested that he was ready to go to prison and death with Jesus. Jesus said, “I tell you, Peter, the cock will not crow this day, until you three times deny that you know me.”\nHours later, in the courtyard of the high priest, Peter denied him three times. The cock crowed, and the Lord turned and looked at Peter. Peter went out and wept bitterly. After the Resurrection, on the shore of Galilee, Jesus asked him three times, “Simon, son of John, do you love me?” and three times commissioned him: “Feed my lambs… Tend my sheep… Feed my sheep.”\nThe man who failed was the man who was given the keys. The Church sees in Peter’s story that infallibility is a gift of Christ’s prayer, not a sign of Peter’s own strength.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write on the board, “The Pope can never be wrong about anything.” Invite students to vote true or false by raising hands, then ask someone from each side to give a reason. Keep the tally for the end.",
    teach=["Say: “Infallibility is the gift by which the Church cannot err in teaching what must be believed about faith or morals. It rests on Christ’s promises: ‘the gates of hell shall not prevail against it’ (Matthew 16:18); ‘I will pray the Father, and he will give you another Counselor, to be with you for ever, the Spirit of truth’ (John 14:16-17); ‘The Spirit of truth will guide you into all the truth.’ (John 16:13) If Christ founded the Church to teach, and if she could teach error about salvation, she would be useless. Paul calls the Church ‘the pillar and bulwark of the truth.’ (1 Timothy 3:15)”", "Say: “Vatican I (1870) defined papal infallibility: ‘when he speaks ex cathedra, that is, when in discharge of the office of pastor and teacher of all Christians, in virtue of his supreme apostolic authority, he defines a doctrine concerning faith or morals to be held by the whole Church,’ he possesses that infallibility with which Christ willed his Church to be endowed. Conditions: he speaks as pastor of all; on faith or morals; definitively; intending to bind the whole Church. Examples: the Immaculate Conception (Pius IX, 1854) and the Assumption (Pius XII, 1950). Ex cathedra definitions are very rare. Infallibility also belongs to the whole Church (the body of the faithful), and to the college of bishops in union with the Pope (e.g., in an ecumenical council or by the ordinary universal Magisterium).”", "Say: “What infallibility is not. It doesn’t mean impeccability (the Pope is a sinner and goes to confession). It doesn’t mean the Pope is inspired (the Holy Spirit assists but does not give new revelation). It doesn’t cover everything he says: his opinions on science, politics or history, his homilies and interviews, his prudential decisions. It doesn’t mean there haven’t been bad popes: some were bad men, yet none officially taught heresy in an ex cathedra definition. Critics point to Honorius, Galileo and the Inquisition. The Church’s response: Honorius did not define a doctrine; the Galileo case was a disciplinary decision of a Roman congregation, not an ex cathedra definition. The charism protects the content of the faith, not the personal wisdom of every pope.”"],
    ask=["Who prayed that Peter’s faith not fail? (Jesus.)", "What are conditions for ex cathedra teaching? (As pastor of all, on faith or morals, definitively, to bind the whole Church.)", "Does infallibility mean the Pope can’t sin? (No.)"],
    practice=["Prepare cards of statements: a Pope’s statement on climate policy; the definition of the Assumption; a Pope’s homily; the Nicene Creed; an encyclical on social justice.", "Sort into infallible, authoritative but not infallible, and personal opinion.", "Write the condition that decides each.", "Write a one-paragraph reply to someone who says ‘Catholics think the Pope can’t be wrong about anything.’"],
    close="Pray: “Lord, keep your Church in the truth.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for the Pope by name.”")),

'10-6-2': dict(
  saint="Thomas Becket: an archbishop who put conscience and the Church before the king.",
  tryit=["Work out a case with the steps of conscience.", "Find where the Church’s teaching enters.", "Decide and defend."],
  saintStory="Thomas Becket was born in London around 1118, the son of a merchant. He became a clerk, then the chancellor of England, and a close friend of King Henry II. They hunted, feasted and worked together. In 1162, Henry made him archbishop of Canterbury, hoping he would serve royal interests in the Church.\nThe change surprised everyone. Thomas gave up his luxury and took up a simple life of prayer and penance. He refused to agree to the king’s Constitutions of Clarendon, which would have placed the clergy under royal courts. The quarrel grew bitter. Thomas fled to France for six years. He returned in 1170 after a truce.\nHenry, in a fit of anger, is said to have exclaimed, “Will no one rid me of this turbulent priest?” Four knights went to Canterbury and killed Thomas in the cathedral on December 29, 1170, as he was walking into evening prayer. Henry did public penance at Thomas’s tomb. Thomas was canonized in 1173.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say, “You are given an order by someone with power over you. You believe it is wrong. What do you do?” Take answers. “This is the problem of conscience and authority.”",
    teach=["Say: “Conscience is the inner judgment of reason that a particular act is right or wrong. The Catechism: ‘Moral conscience, present at the heart of the person, enjoins him at the appropriate moment to do good and to avoid evil.’ (CCC 1777) Vatican II: ‘In the depths of his conscience, man detects a law which he does not impose upon himself, but which holds him to obedience. Its voice… tells him to love and to do what is good and to avoid evil.’ (GS 16) ‘A man has the right to act in conscience and in freedom so as personally to make moral decisions. He must not be forced to act contrary to his conscience.’ (CCC 1782) So we must always follow our conscience, even a mistaken one, if sincere.”", "Say: “But conscience must be formed. It is not simply feelings or opinions. John Henry Newman: conscience is ‘the aboriginal Vicar of Christ,’ but not self-will. He wrote that he would toast ‘to conscience first, and to the Pope afterwards,’ meaning a well-formed conscience. The Church teaches that the Catholic faithful ‘must carefully attend to the sacred and certain doctrine of the Church’ in forming conscience. (Dignitatis Humanae 14) The Church’s teaching authority doesn’t replace conscience; it informs it. When the Church teaches on faith and morals, a Catholic should give assent. If one struggles, one should study, pray, and consult a wise priest or teacher, not simply decide against the Church.”", "Say: “What about a conflict? Conscience can be erroneous: invincibly (through no fault of one’s own), when the person is not culpable; or vincibly, when it results from negligence or sin. We are responsible for forming it well. Conscience can be formed by Scripture, the Magisterium, prayer, advice, examination. It can be deadened by habitual sin. A moral decision involves three sources: the object, the intention and the circumstances. Some acts are intrinsically evil: always wrong, regardless of intention. A good intention does not justify an evil means. Becket and More acted from well-formed conscience in conflict with civil law. We should speak of ‘conscientious objection’ in medicine, military service and so on; the Church supports it.”"],
    ask=["What does the Church teach about following conscience? (Always follow it, but form it well.)", "Can conscience be mistaken? (Yes, and we are responsible for forming it.)", "What are the three sources of morality? (Object, intention, circumstances.)"],
    practice=["Present a case: a nurse is asked to assist in an act the Church teaches is gravely wrong.", "Work through: What is the act? What does the Church teach? What are my options? What advice do I need? What would a well-formed conscience decide?", "Write your response.", "Discuss how conscience relates to law, work and loyalty."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, give me a well-formed conscience and courage to act on it.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Examine your conscience nightly this week.”")),

'10-6-3': dict(
  saint="Alphonsus Liguori: a lawyer who found the path between harshness and laxity.",
  tryit=["Distinguish dissent, doubt and loyal questioning.", "Mark acceptable responses.", "Write what faithful reform looks like."],
  saintStory="Alphonsus Liguori was born in 1696 in Naples, to a noble family. He became a lawyer at sixteen, with a brilliant career. In 1723, he lost a major case, due to missing a document, and was so humiliated that he left the law. He became a priest in 1726 and worked among the poor of Naples. In 1732, he founded the Redemptorists, to preach to abandoned country people.\nHe saw that confessors were tangled in two errors: rigorism (making the moral law impossibly heavy, driving people away) and laxism (excusing everything). He wrote a Moral Theology that took a sensible, pastoral middle way, always looking for the truth but remembering that confession was a hospital, not a court. His principle: when the law truly doubtful, the person is free.\nHe was named bishop at sixty-six and spent his last years in pain, and wrote many books, including The Glories of Mary and Visits to the Blessed Sacrament. He died in 1787 and was declared a Doctor of the Church in 1871. He is the patron of confessors and moral theologians.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What is the difference between asking a hard question and rejecting an answer? Between criticizing a friend and betraying a friend?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Catholics can have difficulties with Church teaching. The Catechism distinguishes. Doubt: honest difficulty in understanding or believing. Involuntary doubt isn’t a sin; voluntary doubt, if deliberately encouraged, is. The father in Mark 9:24 prays, ‘I believe; help my unbelief!’ Questions are part of the search: Thomas Aquinas began each article with objections. Dissent: publicly rejecting a defined teaching. Heresy is the obstinate denial or doubt after Baptism of a truth to be believed with divine and Catholic faith. Different from dissent are theological questions raised to deepen understanding.”", "Say: “The Church asks different levels of assent. For dogmas: the assent of faith. For definitive teachings: firm acceptance. For authoritative non-definitive teaching: ‘religious submission of mind and will’ (LG 25), which is a presumption in favor, with room for serious study. The Church distinguishes between a legitimate theological discussion and public dissent that undermines the faith of others. In 1990, the instruction Donum Veritatis addressed the vocation of theologians: they serve the Church, and difficulties should be raised privately and respectfully with Church authorities. Silencing has happened too: some theologians, like Yves Congar and John Courtney Murray, were censored in the 1950s, and later vindicated at Vatican II. So the Church does recognize that questioning has led to growth.”", "Say: “Reform. The Church is always in need of reform (ecclesia semper reformanda). How? By the saints: Francis, Dominic, Catherine of Siena, Teresa of Avila, Ignatius, Charles Borromeo, Alphonsus. Not by tearing the Church down, but by holiness, charity and fidelity, speaking the truth with love. Catherine rebuked the pope, but kneeling. Her love for the Church was clear. If we see problems, we should pray, study, speak respectfully to those in authority, support good causes, and keep our own lives holy. Newman, Congar and de Lubac suffered misunderstanding but remained faithful. The Church has also reformed discipline: the liturgy, canon law, the handling of abuse. Reform strengthens; dissent can divide.”"],
    ask=["What is the difference between doubt and dissent? (Doubt is an honest difficulty; dissent is public rejection of a teaching.)", "What level of assent does non-definitive teaching call for? (Religious submission of mind and will.)", "How have saints reformed the Church? (By holiness, charity and fidelity.)"],
    practice=["Prepare cases: a student struggles with a teaching; a theologian publishes a dissenting article; a parish group wants the Church to change a discipline; a saint rebukes a bishop in a letter.", "Rate each: faithful questioning, dissent, or reform.", "Write what you would do if you struggled with a teaching.", "Share with a partner."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, renew your Church, beginning with me.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Write down a hard question and find a wise person to discuss it.”")),

'10-7-1': dict(
  saint="Louis de Montfort: a preacher who taught that the shortest way to Jesus is through Mary.",
  tryit=["Trace the four Marian dogmas to their sources.", "Write the Scripture behind each.", "Explain why each is about Christ."],
  saintStory="Louis-Marie Grignion was born in 1673 in Brittany, France. He was ordained in 1700, and became a wandering missionary, preaching in villages and cities, often in the open air and in poverty. He was sometimes banned from preaching in a diocese because of opposition. He rebuilt chapels, taught the Rosary, set up schools, and composed hymns.\nIn about 1712, he wrote True Devotion to Mary, which taught total consecration to Jesus through Mary: giving oneself entirely to Jesus by giving oneself to his mother. The manuscript was hidden in a chest, and not found until 1842. It has since become a classic and influenced Pope John Paul II, whose motto, Totus Tuus, comes from it.\nHe founded the Company of Mary and the Daughters of Wisdom. He died in 1716, at forty-three, and was canonized in 1947. His central point: Marian devotion exists to lead us to Christ.",
  script=dict(
    open="Place a statue or picture of Mary on a table with a candle. Say nothing for thirty seconds. Then ask, “What do you notice about how she is shown? Whom is she looking at, or pointing to?”",
    teach=["Say: “Mary is central to Catholic faith because of who her Son is. All Marian doctrines are ultimately about Christ. There are four Marian dogmas. One: Mary is the Mother of God (Theotokos), defined at Ephesus in 431. Luke 1:43: Elizabeth calls her ‘the mother of my Lord.’ Two: Mary’s perpetual virginity: before, during and after the birth of Christ (Lateran 649). Isaiah 7:14; Luke 1:34. Three: the Immaculate Conception: Mary was preserved from original sin from the first moment of her conception, in view of Christ’s merits (Pius IX, 1854). The angel calls her ‘full of grace,’ kecharitomene (Luke 1:28). Four: the Assumption: at the end of her life, she was taken body and soul into heavenly glory (Pius XII, 1950).”", "Say: “The ‘brothers of the Lord’ in the Gospels: the Church understands them as relatives, because the Greek word and Hebrew ‘ach’ can mean cousin, and the Gospels do not call them Mary’s sons. Mary’s title ‘New Eve’ is found in Irenaeus and Justin. Her role at Cana (John 2) shows her intercession. In Revelation 12, the woman clothed with the sun is read as Mary and the Church. The Church distinguishes veneration (hyperdulia, special honor) given to Mary, from adoration (latria), given to God alone. Catholics do not worship Mary. Mary points to Jesus: ‘Do whatever he tells you.’ (John 2:5)”", "Say: “Lumen Gentium chapter 8 places Mary in the mystery of Christ and the Church. She is Mother of the Church (Paul VI, 1964) and the model disciple. She prays for us as our spiritual mother. At the cross, Jesus said to John, ‘Behold, your mother.’ Marian devotions include the Rosary, the Angelus, the Memorare, the Litany of Loreto, the scapular and consecration. Marian apparitions such as Guadalupe, Lourdes and Fatima are approved as worthy of belief but are not required. The Church’s greatest Marian writing is the Magnificat, Mary’s own song (Luke 1:46-55). Louis de Montfort’s motto: ‘Ad Jesum per Mariam’, to Jesus through Mary.”"],
    ask=["Name the four Marian dogmas. (Mother of God, perpetual virginity, Immaculate Conception, Assumption.)", "What is the difference between veneration and adoration? (Veneration is honor; adoration is for God alone.)", "What does Mary always point to? (Jesus.)"],
    practice=["Make a table of the four dogmas: statement, Bible text, date defined, what it says about Christ.", "Add the Magnificat and underline two lines.", "Write a short reply to a friend who says Catholics worship Mary.", "Pray the Memorare."],
    close="Pray the Memorare together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray the Rosary with your family.”")),

'10-7-2': dict(
  saint="Anthony of Padua: a preacher whom people trust to help them find what is lost.",
  tryit=["Explain the three parts of the Church: pilgrim, suffering, triumphant.", "Describe how the saints help us.", "Write a prayer asking a saint for help."],
  saintStory="Fernando was born in 1195 in Lisbon, to a noble family. At fifteen he joined the Augustinian canons, and studied the Bible. When the bodies of five Franciscan friars martyred in Morocco were brought to Portugal in 1220, he was moved, and he joined the Franciscans, taking the name Anthony. He wanted to go to Morocco himself, but fell ill and was shipped back, and landed in Sicily.\nHis gifts were hidden until, at an ordination, no one else was prepared to preach, and Anthony was asked. He spoke with such depth that Francis of Assisi appointed him to teach his friars. He then preached across Italy and southern France, drawing huge crowds, especially against heresy. He was called the “Hammer of Heretics,” and was known for tenderness to the poor.\nHe died in 1231 at thirty-six and was canonized within a year. A popular story says a novice took his psalter and ran off; Anthony prayed, and the novice returned it, which is why he is invoked to find lost things.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a photo of someone you love who has died, or ask students to picture one. Say, “Are they gone, or just out of sight? What do Catholics say, and why?” Give a minute of silence before any answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church is one family in three states: the Church militant or pilgrim, on earth; the Church suffering, those in purgatory being purified; and the Church triumphant, the saints in heaven. We are united by love. The Catechism says: ‘We believe in the communion of all the faithful of Christ, those who are pilgrims on earth, the dead who are being purified, and the blessed in heaven, all together forming one Church.’ (CCC 962) Death does not break the bonds. Hebrews 12:1: ‘We are surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses.’ The saints in heaven are alive in Christ. Jesus said, ‘God is not God of the dead, but of the living.’”", "Say: “Why do Catholics ask the saints to pray? The same reason we ask friends: James 5:16, ‘The prayer of a righteous man has great power in its effects.’ In Revelation 5:8, the elders offer the prayers of the saints before the Lamb. In 2 Maccabees 15:12-14, Jeremiah prays for the people after his death. At the Transfiguration, Moses and Elijah talk with Jesus. The saints don’t replace Christ as mediator (1 Timothy 2:5), but share in his intercession as members of his body. We pray to God and ask the saints to pray with us. Veneration of saints is not worship. We honor them, imitate them, and ask their prayers. We use statues and icons as reminders, as people use photographs.”", "Say: “Purgatory is the state of purification after death for those who die in God’s friendship but not yet fully purified. 1 Corinthians 3:15 speaks of being saved ‘as through fire.’ The Church prays for the dead (2 Maccabees 12:45-46). We pray for the dead at Mass, on All Souls’ Day (November 2), and we can offer indulgences, Masses and good works. An indulgence is the remission before God of the temporal punishment due to sin whose guilt has already been forgiven; it is granted by the Church under certain conditions. The saints are canonized after a process: a Servant of God, then Venerable, then Blessed (after a miracle), then Saint (after a second miracle; martyrs need none for beatification). Canonization is an infallible declaration that the person is in heaven and can be venerated.”"],
    ask=["What are the three parts of the Church? (Pilgrim on earth, suffering in purgatory, triumphant in heaven.)", "Why ask the saints to pray? (Same as asking friends; they are alive in Christ.)", "What is canonization? (A declaration that a person is in heaven.)"],
    practice=["Draw a diagram of the three states of the Church with arrows of prayer between them.", "Choose a saint for a particular need and write a prayer asking for their help.", "Look up the steps from Servant of God to Saint, and write them.", "Pray for someone who has died."],
    close="Pray the Litany of the Saints (a few invocations) or “Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for your deceased relatives by name.”")),

'10-7-3': dict(
  saint="Titus Brandsma: a Dutch Carmelite professor who defended the free press and died at Dachau.",
  tryit=["Read Hebrews 11 and make your own roll call.", "Write what each hero risked.", "Add a modern hero."],
  saintStory="Titus Brandsma was born in 1881 in the Netherlands, in a farming family. He became a Carmelite priest, a philosopher and professor at the Catholic University of Nijmegen, and a journalist. He was known for his sense of humor, his devotion to Mary and his kindness.\nWhen the Nazis occupied the Netherlands in 1940, they pressured the Catholic press to publish Nazi propaganda. Titus, as the bishops’ adviser to Catholic journalists, traveled from newspaper to newspaper urging editors to refuse. He was arrested in January 1942 and held in prison, then sent to Dachau concentration camp.\nThere he was beaten and experimented on. Even so, he kept up the spirits of fellow prisoners and forgave his guards. A nurse who gave him the lethal injection in July 1942 was given a rosary by him, and wrote later that she was moved by his kindness. On his prison wall he left a note: “Let us pray for those who persecute us.” He died on July 26, 1942, and was canonized in 2022.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Who is a person in history or today that you admire for standing up when it was costly? What did they do?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Read Hebrews 11. Say: “This chapter is a roll call of heroes of faith. ‘By faith Abel offered… By faith Noah… By faith Abraham obeyed… By faith Moses… Rahab… and what more shall I say? Time would fail me to tell of Gideon, Barak, Samson, Jephthah, David, Samuel and the prophets.’ Then comes the point: ‘Therefore, since we are surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses, let us also lay aside every weight and sin which clings so closely, and let us run with perseverance the race that is set before us, looking to Jesus the pioneer and perfecter of our faith.’ (Hebrews 12:1-2)”", "Say: “The Church honors heroes of faith in each age. In the early Church, martyrs. In the Middle Ages, monks and mystics. In the modern age, missionaries, founders, workers for the poor. In the twentieth century, martyrs under Nazism and Communism: Maximilian Kolbe, Edith Stein, Titus Brandsma, Franz Jägerstätter, Cardinal Mindszenty, Oscar Romero, and the martyrs of Mexico. John Paul II said the twentieth century had more Christian martyrs than any before. Heroes also include ordinary people: Pier Giorgio Frassati, Carlo Acutis, Chiara Badano, Gianna Molla, Josemaría. Holiness in every state and every age.”", "Say: “What makes a hero of faith? Not perfection, but love of Christ and others, perseverance, and trust. The saints had flaws; Paul persecuted Christians, Augustine lived badly, Peter denied Jesus. They were changed by grace. Pope Francis wrote in Gaudete et Exsultate (2018) about ‘the saints next door’: ‘the middle class of holiness.’ Saints in the Beatitudes: poor in spirit, meek, merciful, peacemakers. The call to holiness is yours. Leon Bloy said, ‘The only real sadness, the only real failure, the only great tragedy in life, is not to become a saint.’ Whose example will you follow?”"],
    ask=["What does Hebrews 11 describe? (Heroes of faith from the Old Testament.)", "Name a modern martyr. (Kolbe, Brandsma, Romero, Stein.)", "What does Francis call ordinary holiness? (The saints next door.)"],
    practice=["Read Hebrews 11 in a group, each taking a verse.", "On a sheet, make your own roll call with five saints or heroes you know, and a line for what each risked.", "Add one living person who inspires you.", "Write one thing you can imitate."],
    close="Pray the Litany of the Saints or “All you holy men and women, pray for us.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read the life of a saint this week.”")),

'10-8-1': dict(
  saint="Philip the Evangelist: the deacon who baptized the Ethiopian on a desert road.",
  tryit=["Walk through the rite of Baptism.", "Match each sign to Romans 6.", "Renew your promises."],
  saintStory="Philip was one of the seven deacons chosen in Jerusalem (Acts 6). After Stephen’s martyrdom, the persecution scattered the Christians, and Philip went to Samaria, where he preached and many believed.\nAn angel then told him to go south, on the desert road from Jerusalem to Gaza. There he saw an Ethiopian official, the treasurer of Queen Candace, riding in his chariot, reading aloud from the prophet Isaiah. The Spirit said, “Go over and join this chariot.” Philip ran up and asked, “Do you understand what you are reading?” The man said, “How can I, unless someone guides me?” and invited Philip to sit with him. The passage was Isaiah 53, “Like a sheep he was led to the slaughter.” Philip began with that Scripture and told him the good news of Jesus.\nAs they went along, they came to some water. The Ethiopian said, “See, here is water! What is to prevent my being baptized?” They went down into the water and Philip baptized him. Then the Spirit carried Philip away, and the eunuch went on his way rejoicing.",
  script=dict(
    open="Fill a clear bowl with water and set it where all can see. Say, “Water can drown, wash, and give life. In Baptism it does all three at once.” Ask three volunteers to each name one of those uses in daily life.",
    teach=["Read Romans 6:3-11. Say: “‘Do you not know that all of us who have been baptized into Christ Jesus were baptized into his death? We were buried therefore with him by baptism into death, so that as Christ was raised from the dead by the glory of the Father, we too might walk in newness of life.’ The Paschal Mystery is Christ’s passing from death to life: his Passion, death, Resurrection and Ascension. We share in it in Baptism. Going into the water is a burial; coming out is rising. The Church’s whole sacramental life flows from Christ’s Pasch. This is why the Easter Vigil is the great night of Baptisms.”", "Say: “What does Baptism do? It forgives original sin and all personal sin. It makes us children of God, members of Christ and temples of the Holy Spirit. It incorporates us into the Church. It gives sanctifying grace and the theological virtues. It imprints an indelible character, which is why it cannot be repeated. The rite has signs: water, the sign of the cross, the oil of catechumens, the profession of faith, the white garment, the lighted candle. Infant Baptism: the Church has baptized infants since apostolic times (Acts 16:15, 33; 1 Corinthians 1:16 refer to ‘households’), on the basis of faith of the parents and godparents, as circumcision was done to infants. The parents promise to raise the child in the faith.”", "Say: “Baptism of desire and blood: someone who dies for Christ before being baptized, or someone who desires Baptism but dies before receiving it, can be saved. Adult converts go through the Rite of Christian Initiation of Adults (RCIA), receiving Baptism, Confirmation and Eucharist at the Easter Vigil. Baptism in other Christian communities is valid if done with water and the Trinitarian formula; so converts from other Christian groups are not rebaptized. We renew our promises at Easter: ‘Do you reject Satan? And all his works? And all his empty promises?’ In daily life, dying to sin means turning away from selfishness; rising means a new life of love. We make the Sign of the Cross with holy water as a reminder.”"],
    ask=["What does Baptism do? (Forgives sin; makes us children of God and members of the Church; gives grace.)", "Why can Baptism be received only once? (It leaves an indelible mark.)", "Why do we baptize infants? (Apostolic practice; faith of the parents and Church.)"],
    practice=["Create a flowchart of the rite of Baptism with all signs: Sign of the Cross, readings, anointing, blessing of the water, renunciation and profession of faith, pouring of water, chrism, white garment, candle.", "Beside each, write what it means and the Romans 6 connection.", "Write your own baptismal date and godparents, if known.", "Renew your promises together."],
    close="Renew the baptismal promises. Bless yourselves with water. Send home: “Find your baptismal certificate or candle.”")),

'10-8-2': dict(
  saint="Pius X: the pope who welcomed children to Communion and urged frequent reception.",
  tryit=["List the Eucharist’s three dimensions.", "Match each to a part of the Mass.", "Write what it asks of the participant."],
  saintStory="Giuseppe Sarto was born in 1835 in a poor village in northern Italy, the son of a postman and seamstress. He became a priest, a pastor, a bishop and a cardinal-patriarch of Venice. When he went to Rome for the conclave of 1903, he bought a round-trip ticket, expecting to return. Instead, he was elected pope, Pius X. He said, “I was born poor, I lived poor, I will die poor.”\nHe made the Eucharist central. In 1905 he encouraged frequent and even daily Communion, which had become rare because of an excessive sense of unworthiness. In 1910, in the decree Quam Singulari, he lowered the age for First Communion to about seven, the age of reason, so that children could receive Jesus early. He also restored Gregorian chant to the liturgy, promoted the study of the Catechism, and reformed the Roman Curia and canon law.\nHe died in 1914, shortly after the outbreak of World War I, and was canonized in 1954.",
  script=dict(
    open="Read aloud Luke 22:19-20 slowly. Pause and say, “Notice the verbs: took, blessed, broke, gave. Those four actions are still the shape of every Mass.” Ask students to listen for them in the next Mass they attend.",
    teach=["Say: “The Mass makes present the Paschal Mystery. At the Last Supper, Jesus anticipated his death by giving his Body and Blood under bread and wine. On the cross, he offered himself. The Council of Trent said that in the Mass ‘that same Christ is contained and offered in an unbloody manner who offered himself once in a bloody manner on the altar of the cross.’ It’s not a new sacrifice nor a repetition. There is one sacrifice, and the Mass makes it present in every time and place. Hebrews teaches that Christ offered himself ‘once for all.’ (Hebrews 10:10) The Eucharistic Prayer’s anamnesis (remembrance) is not merely recalling but making present.”", "Say: “The Eucharist has three dimensions. Sacrifice: Christ offers himself to the Father, and the Church offers with him and in him. This is why we say the priest offers the sacrifice ‘in persona Christi,’ and the people offer too. We offer ourselves: our work, joys, sufferings. Real presence: Christ is present under the appearances of bread and wine, body, blood, soul and divinity. ‘This is my body.’ Communion: we receive him, and are united with him and one another. Paul: ‘Because there is one bread, we who are many are one body, for we all partake of the one bread.’ (1 Corinthians 10:17) The Church makes the Eucharist and the Eucharist makes the Church.”", "Say: “Related teaching. Transubstantiation: the substance of bread and wine is changed into the substance of Christ’s body and blood, while the appearances remain. Trent used this term. Eucharistic worship outside Mass: the reserved Sacrament, adoration, Benediction, processions. Obligation: Catholics must attend Mass on Sundays and holy days, and receive Communion at least once a year at Easter. Worthy reception requires the state of grace, a one-hour fast, and reverence. Intercommunion: because Communion signifies full unity in faith, the Church normally reserves Communion to Catholics, with exceptions for Orthodox and others in special cases. The Church also reveres the Eucharist as the ‘source and summit of the Christian life.’ A mature Catholic lives from the Mass.”"],
    ask=["Is the Mass a new sacrifice? (No; it makes the one sacrifice of Calvary present.)", "What are the three dimensions? (Sacrifice, real presence, communion.)", "What is transubstantiation? (The substance changes; the appearances remain.)"],
    practice=["Make a three-column table: Sacrifice, Presence, Communion.", "Under each, write the parts of the Mass that show it, a Scripture text, and what it asks of me.", "Write a prayer to say before Mass.", "Attend Mass this week with one intention in mind."],
    close="Pray the Anima Christi. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Prepare for Sunday Mass by reading the Gospel.”")),

'10-8-3': dict(
  saint="Cardinal Nguyễn Văn Thuận: an archbishop who spent thirteen years in prison and never lost hope.",
  tryit=["Write a plan for a daily prayer rhythm.", "Include the Examen and Scripture.", "List a source of hope in hard times."],
  saintStory="Francis Xavier Nguyễn Văn Thuận was born in 1928 in Vietnam, a family with deep Catholic roots. He became a priest, then bishop of Nha Trang. In April 1975, just days before the fall of Saigon, Pope Paul VI named him coadjutor archbishop of Saigon. The Communists took over, and he was arrested, accused of conspiring with foreign powers. He was imprisoned for thirteen years, nine of them in solitary confinement.\nHe was kept in a cell without a window; the light was kept on all the time. He decided to use each day to love. He secretly celebrated Mass with three drops of wine and a drop of water in the palm of his hand, and a piece of bread, which friends smuggled in as medicine for stomach ache. He made a cross from scraps of wood and wire. His guards, who at first were forbidden to talk to him, became his friends; he taught them French and Latin, and some began to pray.\nReleased in 1988, he was exiled in 1991, and became a cardinal in 2001 and worked at the Vatican on justice and peace. He wrote The Road of Hope and Testimony of Hope. He died in 2002.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say, “Imagine being alone in a room with no windows for a year. What would you do to keep your mind and spirit alive? What would you hold on to?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Christian hope is the theological virtue by which we desire the Kingdom of heaven and eternal life as our happiness, trusting in Christ’s promises and relying not on our own strength but on the help of the Holy Spirit. (CCC 1817) It is not optimism, a feeling that things will turn out okay. It is trust in God who has already won in Christ. Hebrews 6:19: ‘We have this as a sure and steadfast anchor of the soul.’ The Paschal Mystery gives the shape: the cross leads to the Resurrection. Romans 8:24: ‘In this hope we were saved.’ Pope Benedict XVI’s encyclical Spe Salvi (2007) says, ‘The one who has hope lives differently.’”", "Say: “Hope is nourished by prayer. The Catechism calls prayer ‘the school of hope.’ Prayer is the raising of the mind and heart to God. We pray with vocal prayer, meditation and contemplation. We pray with the Psalms, the Our Father, and the Rosary. We pray in the Liturgy of the Hours. We pray with silence and Scripture. A simple daily rhythm: morning offering; grace at meals; some Scripture or a psalm; an Examen in the evening; and a short night prayer. In the Our Father, the seven petitions sum up all hope. In suffering, prayer is a lifeline.”", "Say: “The Church’s hope is also about the last things. Death: the end of earthly life; the soul is judged. Heaven: eternal life with God, the beatific vision, seeing God face to face (1 Corinthians 13:12). Purgatory: purification. Hell: the state of definitive self-exclusion from communion with God, a real possibility because we are free. Final resurrection: our bodies will be raised. New creation: ‘a new heaven and a new earth.’ We live in the ‘already, but not yet.’ Cardinal Thuận wrote, ‘I will not wait. I will live the present moment and fill it to the brim with love.’ Christian hope shapes how we face suffering and death: with prayer and peace. The Church’s rites for the dying, Viaticum and the Anointing, give strength.”"],
    ask=["What is Christian hope? (Trust in God’s promise of eternal life through Christ.)", "What is the beatific vision? (Seeing God face to face in heaven.)", "How does prayer nourish hope? (It keeps us in relationship with God.)"],
    practice=["Design a daily prayer rhythm: morning, midday, evening, night.", "Include at least four elements (Morning Offering, Scripture, grace, Examen).", "List three sources of hope that you can reach in a hard time.", "Share your rhythm with a partner, and commit to trying it for a week."],
    close="Pray the Act of Hope together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Follow your daily prayer plan for a week.”")),

'10-9-1': dict(
  saint="Margaret of Castello: a child abandoned for her disabilities, who became a Dominican sister and a comforter of others.",
  tryit=["List the Church’s reasons for protecting the unborn.", "List the help she offers to mothers.", "Plan one act of support."],
  saintStory="Margaret was born in 1287 in Metola, Italy, to a nobleman and his wife. She was born blind, with a curved spine and a limp, and was very small. Her parents, ashamed of her, hid her away for years in a small room. When she was six, they took her to a shrine, and hoping for a cure, prayed. No miracle came. They left her there and went home.\nMargaret was abandoned in a strange town, and fended for herself as a beggar. Eventually, poor families took her in. She was cheerful and prayed constantly, and taught the children the Psalms, which she had learned by heart. She joined the Dominican laity, and visited prisoners and the sick. People noticed how joyful she was.\nShe died at thirty-three, in 1320. An autopsy was said to reveal three small stones in her heart, each bearing a holy image. In 2021 Pope Francis extended her veneration to the whole Church as a saint. She is invoked as a patron of the unborn and disabled.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say gently and clearly, “This lesson is on one of the most painful and divisive issues of our time. We will speak with truth and compassion. Some of you may have personal experiences. Nobody here will be judged. Let’s start with the question: what makes a human being valuable?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Catholic Church teaches that human life is sacred and must be respected from the moment of conception. The Catechism: ‘Human life must be respected and protected absolutely from the moment of conception. From the first moment of his existence, a human being must be recognized as having the rights of a person—among which is the inviolable right of every innocent being to life.’ (CCC 2270) The reasons: Biblical: ‘Before I formed you in the womb I knew you.’ (Jeremiah 1:5; Psalm 139:13-16; Luke 1:41-44, John the Baptist leaps in the womb). Biological: at conception a new, genetically distinct human organism begins. Philosophical: personhood doesn’t depend on size, stage, ability, or dependence. The Didache, from about AD 100: ‘You shall not kill a child by abortion.’”", "Say: “Direct abortion, the deliberate termination of a pregnancy, is gravely wrong. The Church also attaches the penalty of excommunication to those who procure a completed abortion, to emphasize the gravity and to help people see their need for repentance (CCC 2272). The Church distinguishes this from medical treatment to save a mother’s life that has the unintended effect of ending the pregnancy (the principle of double effect). Examples: removing a cancerous uterus or treating an ectopic pregnancy, where the aim is to save the mother and not to end the child’s life. The Church also condemns embryonic stem-cell research that destroys embryos, and in vitro fertilization that creates and discards embryos. In 2022, the U.S. Supreme Court in Dobbs overturned Roe v. Wade, returning the question to the states.”", "Say: “The Church’s teaching is joined with compassion. Many women who choose abortion feel pressured, afraid or abandoned. The Church calls for support. Pregnancy resource centers provide free medical help, counseling, diapers and housing. Adoption is a generous option. Healing is available for those who have had abortions: the Church teaches that God’s mercy is greater than any sin, and the sacrament of Reconciliation is available. Projects such as Project Rachel and Rachel’s Vineyard offer counseling and retreats. What about hard cases? Pregnancy from rape: the Church acknowledges the terrible suffering of the woman and urges compassion and care; the child is innocent and also a victim. A diagnosis of disability: Margaret of Castello shows the worth of a life beyond ability. The Church’s message: love both. ‘Choose life, that you and your descendants may live.’ (Deuteronomy 30:19)”"],
    ask=["What does the Church teach about when life begins? (From conception.)", "What is the principle of double effect? (Allows treatment that saves the mother even if it unintentionally ends the pregnancy.)", "What help does the Church offer after an abortion? (Mercy in Reconciliation, and healing ministries.)"],
    practice=["Make two columns: “Reasons the Church gives” and “Ways to help mothers and children.”", "Fill in with at least five items each.", "Choose one way to help locally (a pregnancy center, a diaper drive, a prayer group).", "Write a prayer for mothers, children and those who have suffered through abortion."],
    close="Pray the Hail Mary for mothers and unborn children. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “If you or someone you know needs help, speak with a trusted adult or priest.”")),

'10-9-2': dict(
  saint="Zélie and Louis Martin: parents of Thérèse, who raised nine children with faith and love.",
  tryit=["Lay out the reasoning of Humanae Vitae.", "Compare methods.", "Write one question and a reply."],
  saintStory="Louis Martin was a watchmaker and Zélie Guérin a lacemaker in Alençon, France. Each had once tried to enter religious life and been refused. They met in 1858 and married three months later. Louis proposed that they live as brother and sister, but a confessor advised them to open themselves to children. They had nine children between 1860 and 1873. Four died in infancy or childhood, which devastated them. Five daughters survived, and all became nuns, including Thérèse of Lisieux.\nThey went to Mass daily, kept the fast, and prayed together. Zélie ran a successful lace business, while caring for children and writing many letters, which survive. In 1877, she died of breast cancer, at forty-five. Louis moved the family to Lisieux and raised the girls. Later he suffered strokes and dementia and was placed in a hospital, which was a great humiliation, and he died in 1894.\nIn 2015, Pope Francis canonized them, the first married couple canonized together. Their story shows marriage as a path to holiness.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What do you think marriage is for? List purposes.” Write them. Circle “love” and “children.” Ask, “Can these be separated? Should they be?”",
    teach=["Say: “Catholic teaching on marriage is that it is a covenant between a man and a woman, ordered to the good of the spouses and the procreation and education of children. These are the two ‘ends,’ or meanings, of marriage: the unitive (love) and the procreative (children). Humanae Vitae (1968), Pope Paul VI’s encyclical, taught that ‘each and every marriage act must remain open to the transmission of life.’ (HV 11) This follows from the inseparable connection between the unitive and procreative meanings of the marital act. It does not mean that every act produces a child, but that couples should not deliberately frustrate it.”", "Say: “Contraception is any action before, during or after intercourse intended to prevent procreation. The Church teaches that it is morally wrong. Reasons: it separates the unitive and procreative meanings that God joined. It says to your spouse, ‘I give you all of me except my fertility.’ It reduces the person to an object to be used. Paul VI predicted consequences: a general lowering of moral standards, marital infidelity, loss of respect for women, and governments imposing birth control. Many point to the sexual revolution as evidence. The Church holds that it’s about total self-giving: marriage is a gift of self. Critics argue the teaching is unrealistic; surveys show most Catholics disagree. The Church doesn’t decide by polls, but she must explain it well.”", "Say: “Responsible parenthood. The Church doesn’t demand maximal numbers of children. Couples can space births or limit family size for serious reasons (health, finances, emotional readiness). They may use Natural Family Planning (NFP): methods based on observing a woman’s natural signs of fertility (cervical mucus, temperature) and abstaining during fertile times if avoiding pregnancy. NFP is different from the old calendar rhythm method. Modern methods such as Creighton, Billings and Symptothermal are more than 95-99 percent effective with correct use in studies. The difference: contraception acts against fertility; NFP cooperates with it. NFP requires communication, self-control and cooperation. Many couples report greater intimacy. NFP is also used to achieve pregnancy and for women’s health. Zélie and Louis show a family’s joy and cost: their trust in God sustained them.”"],
    ask=["What are the two meanings of marriage? (Unitive and procreative.)", "What does NFP do? (Uses natural signs of fertility to space births, cooperating with fertility.)", "Does the Church require couples to have as many children as possible? (No; responsible parenthood allows spacing for serious reasons.)"],
    practice=["Lay out HV’s reasoning in four steps: marriage’s two meanings; their inseparable connection; therefore, no deliberate frustration; therefore NFP.", "Write the strongest objection and a reply.", "Compare NFP and contraception in a chart.", "Write one question you would like to ask a married couple about the teaching."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, bless every married couple and every family.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask a married couple what marriage has taught them.”")),

'10-9-3': dict(
  saint="Maria Goretti: a girl who chose to die rather than sin, and whose attacker was converted by her forgiveness.",
  tryit=["Define chastity in your own words.", "Sort actions into respect and use.", "Write your commitment."],
  saintStory="Maria Goretti was born in 1890 in Italy, to a poor farming family. When she was nine, her father died of malaria, and the family moved to work on a farm with another family, the Serenellis. Maria cooked, cleaned and cared for her younger siblings while her mother worked in the fields. She was devout, and prepared for First Communion at eleven.\nAlessandro Serenelli, a young man of eighteen, began pressing her for sexual favors. She refused. On July 5, 1902, when they were alone in the house, he attacked her. She resisted, crying, “No! It is a sin! God does not want it!” He stabbed her fourteen times. She died the next day in the hospital. Before dying, she forgave him and said she wanted him in heaven with her.\nAlessandro spent twenty-seven years in prison, unrepentant. Then he had a dream in which Maria offered him lilies. He repented and was released. He asked Maria’s mother for forgiveness, and she forgave him. In 1950, Alessandro stood with Maria’s mother at Maria’s canonization. He ended his life as a lay brother with the Capuchins.",
  script=dict(
    open="Be calm and respectful. Say, “This is about a beautiful topic that society often gets wrong. Let’s begin with a question: what is the opposite of love? Is it hate?” Take answers. “Karol Wojtyła, later Pope John Paul II, said the opposite of love is use.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church teaches that sexuality is a gift of God, good and holy, meant for love and life within marriage. The Catechism: ‘Chastity means the successful integration of sexuality within the person and thereby the inner unity of man in his bodily and spiritual being.’ (CCC 2337) Chastity is not repression; it’s self-mastery for the sake of love. It is a virtue for everyone: single people, married people, priests and religious, each according to their state. Married chastity means fidelity. For the unmarried, chastity means abstaining from sexual relations. In dating, it means respecting each other’s dignity and the meaning of the body.”", "Say: “John Paul II developed this in his Theology of the Body (1979-84). The body expresses the person; it has a ‘nuptial meaning,’ the capacity to express love by self-gift. Sex is a total gift of self, ‘I am yours, completely, permanently,’ which is why it belongs to marriage. Outside that, it says something false with the body. He wrote in Love and Responsibility that a person is never to be used merely as a means. Sins against chastity include lust, masturbation, fornication, pornography, prostitution, rape and homosexual acts. Pornography is gravely harmful: it reduces persons to objects, can be addictive, and distorts how we see others. Most people struggle with these things; the Church teaches that God’s mercy is greater, that Confession heals, and that growth in chastity is possible with grace.”", "Say: “What about people with same-sex attraction? The Catechism teaches that homosexual acts are not in accord with the natural law, but that persons with homosexual tendencies ‘must be accepted with respect, compassion, and sensitivity. Every sign of unjust discrimination in their regard should be avoided.’ (CCC 2358) They are called to chastity, like everyone. Practical help: pray; frequent Confession and Mass; avoid occasions of sin (what you watch, who you are alone with); set clear limits in dating; modesty in dress and speech as respect for self and others; friendships; talking to a trusted adult. Chastity and purity are not just about avoiding sin but about positive love. ‘Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.’ (Matthew 5:8)”"],
    ask=["What is chastity? (Integrating sexuality in the person for the sake of love, according to one’s state.)", "What did John Paul II say is the opposite of love? (Use.)", "How does the Church say we should treat persons with same-sex attraction? (With respect, compassion and sensitivity; no unjust discrimination.)"],
    practice=["Draw a line from “use” to “love” and place examples along it: treating a date as a trophy, texting a kind note, pressuring a partner, waiting, pornography, honoring a friend.", "Write your own definition of chastity in your own words.", "Write a short personal commitment and one step to keep it: boundaries, friends, screen habits.", "Keep it private or share with a trusted adult."],
    close="Pray: “Create in me a clean heart, O God.” (Psalm 51:10.) Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Talk with a trusted adult or priest about any questions.”")),
}
